Subject: [PATCH] nested wayland backend: real mode ladder + apply

Quasar patch against kwin 6.7.4 (Fedora 43: kwin-6.7.4-1.fc43).

PROBLEM
-------
KWin's nested `wayland` backend synthesises exactly ONE output mode, from the
size the host compositor configures its xdg_toplevel at:

  * `wayland_output.cpp init()`           -> `modes = {mode}`
  * `wayland_output.cpp applyConfigure()` -> `modes = {mode}`  (configure size *
                                             wp_fractional_scale_v1 scale)
  * `wayland_output.cpp framePresented()` -> `modes = {mode}`  (on refresh change)

and `applyChanges(OutputConfiguration &)` never reads `props->currentMode` at
all (it also has `props->scale` commented out). `Capability::CustomModes` is not
set. So inside a nested Plasma session, System Settings -> Display lists a
single resolution and applying anything is a no-op.

For Quasar the nested session IS the desktop the user streams, so "pick your
internal resolution" has to work from Display Settings.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
1. `wayland_display.{h,cpp}`: bind the HOST compositor's `wl_output` (v2) purely
   to collect its `mode` events, and expose the de-duplicated, largest-first
   ladder as `WaylandDisplay::parentModeSizes()` + a `parentModesChanged()`
   signal. Nothing else in the backend uses wl_output.

2. `wayland_output.{h,cpp}`: build a real `State::modes` list = the
   host-configured size (flagged Preferred) plus every host rung no larger than
   it, with stable `OutputMode` identities (`m_modeCache`) so KScreen does not
   see the list churn. `applyChanges()` now honours `props->currentMode` and
   remembers the pick in `m_userMode`.

3. Stickiness: a user-picked mode is INDEPENDENT of the host's configure size.
   `applyConfigure()` never drops `m_userMode` because the size changed - Quasar
   steps the streamed (external) resolution live, and an external step must not
   throw away the user's chosen internal resolution. The pick only degrades, to
   the new native size, when it no longer FITS inside it. It likewise survives
   the same-size configure the host sends right after a mode change.

4. Fit-to-host: the root surface's `wp_viewport` destination is pinned to the
   host-configured logical size, and the content subsurfaces
   (`wayland_layer.cpp`) are scaled/centred into it by `contentScale()` /
   `contentOffset()` - aspect preserving, letterboxed against the existing black
   root buffer. Pointer input (`wayland_backend.cpp`) and the cursor surface
   (`wayland_egl_backend.cpp`, `wayland_qpainter_backend.cpp`) go through the
   same mapping.

   `contentScale()` is EXACTLY `1 / scale()` and `contentOffset()` exactly (0,0)
   while the output runs at the host-configured size, which is what the
   unpatched code hardcodes - so this is a no-op until a mode or scale is
   actually changed.

5. Scale: `props->scale` is honoured, but ONLY when the host does not implement
   `wp_fractional_scale_v1` (upstream's stated reason for ignoring it is that
   that protocol would overwrite it - true only when the host has it). This is
   what makes the KScreen scale slider do anything under a host that lacks the
   protocol.

6. Rungs: the host's ladder is the primary source, but it is only a HINT - this
   backend always commits a surface covering the host's configured area, so any
   size at or below it is presentable against ANY host. Since every headless
   parent (wlroots, mutter, and kwin's own server, whose `wl_output` only ever
   sends the CURRENT mode) advertises exactly one, the standard rungs in the
   host-configured size's aspect family are offered too. `KWIN_NESTED_EXTRA_MODES=0`
   restricts the list to the host's own ladder.

`Capability::CustomModes` is deliberately still NOT set: the ladder is the
host's, not arbitrary user-defined modelines.

